<section class="content-section" id="about" aria-labelledby="profile-title">
  <div class="about">
    <p class="section-label">About</p>
    <h2 id="profile-title">Everything is open source if you know where to look</h2>
    <p>
      I started my cybersecurity journey early, mainly by hacking into my dad's
      laptop to play video games. I wasn't sure what I wanted to do, so I went to
      college to study computer engineering. I worked in my college's IT department
      for a couple of years, but I wasn't satisfied. Computers were fun, but I
      adored embedded systems, so I made them my concentration.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's when it clicked: I needed to understand exactly how things worked.
      Embedded systems made these concepts easier to grasp because they weren't
      designed to hide everything behind high-level abstractions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Then I was introduced to the SystemsCyber group, which demonstrated a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SwtTzk9ys20">truck drive-by attack</a>.
      You're telling me hacking can have physical consequences?! I was in love.
      I went on to discover vulnerabilities and develop automotive security tooling
      of my own. I now work with automotive systems, preventing <a href="https://autosec-timeline.delikely.eu.org/?lang=en">safety-critical failures</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Why Bottoms Up? The phrase describes raising a glass and emptying its
      contents (typically into one's mouth). When working with systems, you quickly
      become familiar with top-down and bottom-up engineering (my thesis was based on this). I combined the two ideas:
      as much as I like beer, I love reverse engineering
      a system until it becomes open source. Hence the motto, “Everything is open
      source if you know where to look.”
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

